\details{
\emph{Species effects}. A species' response to a numeric covariate is the
average slope of its linear predictor, over the rows the species was
fitted to, as \code{\link[marginaleffects:slopes]{marginaleffects::avg_slopes()}} gives it with
\code{type = "link"}. A linear term gives the species' own coefficient,
such as \code{b_env} plus \code{b_env:seriessp_2} in \code{y ~ env * series}. A
smooth or an interaction gives the slope averaged over the species'
rows. A smooth that rises and falls can average to a slope near zero.
\code{\link[=conditional_effects.mvgam]{conditional_effects.mvgam()}} shows its shape. A covariate in
\code{trend_formula}, including one written with \code{\link[=lv_axis]{lv_axis()}}, reaches each
species through its loadings. A factor covariate gives one effect per
level, its difference from the reference level.

A \code{\link[=diri]{diri()}}, \code{\link[=multi]{multi()}} or \code{\link[=categ]{categ()}} fit gives each species' share
through a softmax, which ignores a slope every species shares. Each
draw is then centred across the species. An effect is the slope of
the species' log share relative to the geometric mean of the shares,
the centred log-ratio scale.

An effect is on the link scale, per unit of the covariate. Effects of
covariates measured in different units compare better when the
covariates are standardised. A covariate that only a distributional
formula uses, such as the detection formula of \code{\link[=occ]{occ()}}, moves no
species' linear predictor and is refused.

\emph{Trait effects}. \code{trait_slopes = ~ trait} in \code{\link[=jsdgam]{jsdgam()}} makes each
species' response depend on its traits. A trait effect is how far the
response moves per unit of the trait, with each species' own deviation
held. A linear term gives its coefficient, such as \code{b_env:size}. A
smooth of the covariate or of the trait gives the effect averaged over
the fitted rows. A factor trait gives one effect per level, its
difference from the reference level. A covariate of \code{factor_formula}
reaches no trait. Its trait effects are zero.

\emph{Support}. \code{prob_nonzero} is the larger of the posterior
probabilities that an effect is positive and that it is negative. An
effect is supported when \code{prob_nonzero} exceeds \code{prob_threshold}.
}
\examples{
\dontrun{
# Detections of 6 species at 80 sites, 4 visits each. Occupancy
# responds to the site covariate `env` and detection to the time of
# day `tod_c`. Two latent factors drive the species associations.
simdat <- sim_closure_unit_data(
  family    = occ(),
  n_species = 6L,
  n_sites   = 80L,
  n_visits  = 4L,
  n_lv      = 2L,
  seed      = 1L
)

# A joint occupancy model. Each species has its own occupancy
# intercept and `env` slope, plus its own detection intercept. Two
# latent factors take up the residual association.
mod <- jsdgam(
  formula        = bf(y ~ series * env, p ~ series + tod_c),
  factor_formula = ~ -1,
  data           = simdat$data_train,
  family         = occ(),
  n_lv           = 2L,
  chains         = 2,
  silent         = 2
)

summary(mod)

# The effect of `env` on the chance of a detection, occupancy times
# detection
conditional_effects(mod)

# Each species' response to env, outlined where its sign is clear
plot(species_effects(mod, variables = "env"))

# The residual correlations among species the factors imply
plot(residual_cor(mod))

# Site scores and species loadings on the two factors
ordinate(mod, rotation = "varimax")

# Expected richness along `env`. The second plot marks the pairs of
# species found together more often than `env` predicts.
gradient <- datagrid(model = mod, env = seq(-2, 2, length.out = 20))
cm <- community(mod, newdata = gradient)
plot(cm)
plot(cm, type = "cooccurrence")

# The draws, for any contrast: richness at the end of the gradient
# less that at its start
rv <- as_draws_rvars(cm)
rv$richness[20] - rv$richness[1]

# Next, compare n_lv = 1 with update(mod, n_lv = 1L) and loo()
}

}
